 > ... else if (Blind) {
 > goto skip
 > }

Notice first that "Blind" is a global only
pertaining to the PC, so this is not where
monster blindness or sightedness is accomplished
in any case, nor where its effects are calculated.

This still assumes that there is one and only one
"retained image" of how the level is lighted. Since
your problem seems to be that monsters see the level
differently than the blind or out of line of sight
PC remembers the level. You can't fix your perceived
problem until you change that situation to create
instead multiple such images of how the level is
seen / remembered, one for the PC, possibly as many
as one additional for each monster on the level,
possibly inconsistent with one another as needed,
and do all the work to do needed maintenance on all
those levels for every change which might be
visible, in synchrony but as modified by visual
issues for each monster involved including the PC.

You do realize that since any monster can be blind
by nature, blind by situation, or sighted and
seeing, your fix will require that at _every_
movement, "lightedness" will have to be separately
calculated for _every_ monster on the level
including the PC?

That calculation might well come out different
in every single case. It probably will, in fact,
because line of sight comes into play for each
separate monster as well.

Once locations' lightedness is computed, the monster
must do such actions as its awareness of lighting
and the things lighting implies influences.

Now consider that one must interleave _that_ fix
with NetHack's baroque movement / turn system which
creates the possibility that a "turn" can be chopped
into up to "very-fast" movement increments, 24 if I
remember correctly.

You're going to need a pretty fast processor to make
all that work possible at a reasonable game play
pace.

You'll also have to split Blind into u.Blind and
however one spells m.Blind for each monster,
requiring a change in the struct for the PC and the
struct for monsters, perhaps not a fun thing to do
either.

Notice also (as said once above) that you'll now
need to have separate image copies of the level at
least for "PC remembers" and "monster sees", since
you will have to get back to the "PC remembers"
version any time it is the turn of the PC, and
always before refreshing the display of the map to
the player.

If you intend for monsters to behave
quasi-intelligently, then _each_ monster would have
to have a _retained_ pair of "monster sees" and
"monster remembers" images of the level for the
situation where it wanders around sighted but sees
different parts of the level than does the PC, and
for where it goes blind while alive and must
remember what it last knew, so that it could, for
example, run back and pick up a weapon previously
seen in the lamplight of a passing PC if it loses
its current weapon.

Of course, meanwhile, both the lighting may have
changed and the weapon may have been displaced.

Probably all this would need to be special cased
again for monsters which happen to be pets,
peaceful, or aggressive, since their behavior in
respect to objects seen differs.

I'm not saying don't fix the problem, if it annoys
you or breaks some tournament challenge, I'm just
suggesting that you look at how incredibly nasty the
fix will be to accomplish correctly and considering
all the corner cases and major issue involved.

It might be easiest to go back and redesign the game
from scratch to fix something this major. That would
be the time to switch to a platform neutral object
oriented language.

I'm sure, as I'm not very familiar with the NetHack
source code, most of which I last looked at more
than six years ago, that I've missed _dozens_ more
_major_ issues that such a fix will have to get
right. You might want to ask people smarter about
the code than I am to suggest what some of those
issues might be.

